<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Otto I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">crowned <a href="/years/962">962 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Otto I of Saxony · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottonian-dynasty">Ottonian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-the-illustrious">Otto I, Duke of Saxony</a> and King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, was crowned emperor by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-xii">Pope John XII</a> in 962 CE. He had earlier defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lechfeld">Battle of Lechfeld</a> in 955. His coronation is the date most historians treat as the functional founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/otto-i.jpg" alt="Otto I" width="320" height="310" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Otto Posse · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siegel_Otto_I_Posse.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 936 Abd al-Rahman III began building the palace city of Madinat al-Zahra west of Córdoba, where foreign envoys, among them embassies from Byzantium and from <b>Otto I</b> of Germany, were received with elaborate ritual.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caliphate-of-cordoba">Caliphate of Córdoba · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In 959 Archbishop Bruno of Cologne, brother of <b>Otto I</b> and duke of Lotharingia, divided it into Lower Lorraine in the north and Upper Lorraine along the Moselle and upper Meuse, entrusting the south to Frederick, count of Bar.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Duchy of Lorraine · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under <b>Otto I</b> the Saxon frontier became a system of marches: Hermann Billung commanded on the lower Elbe and Margrave Gero in the middle Elbe lands, imposing tribute on the Slavs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the fragmentation of the Carolingian realm the imperial succession lapsed and was contested until <b>Otto I of Saxony</b>, having defeated the Magyars at the Battle of Lechfeld in 955 CE, was crowned emperor by Pope John XII in 962 CE, the date most historians treat as the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Duchy of Saxony, ruled by the Liudolfing family, produced the kings of East Francia from 919, beginning with Henry the Fowler, and his son <b>Otto I</b> was crowned emperor in 962.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saxons">Saxons · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Near Augsburg, King <b>Otto I</b>, outnumbered nearly two to one, began three days of fighting that ended in the defeat of the Hungarian army led by Bulcsú, Lél and Súr. The victory ended the Magyar raids into the German lands.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-10">10 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
